SU(1)                          P32W Unix                           SU(1)

NAME
       su - become another user

SYNOPSIS
       su [-] [user] [-c command]

DESCRIPTION
       su runs a new shell with the user and group ids of user,
       root if none is given. Unless you are already root, or the
       account has no password, su turns echo off and asks for that
       user's password (Password: ). A wrong one gets Sorry.

       The shell is the user's shell from /etc/passwd (/bin/sh if
       the field is empty). Without -, the current directory and
       the environment (HOME, USER, PATH ...) are left as they
       were. Exit the shell (exit or ^D) to return to the original
       user.

       su is set-user-id root (/bin/su, mode 4755), which lets it
       change ids.

OPTIONS
       -  (or -l)
              a login environment: HOME, USER, LOGNAME and SHELL
              are set for the new user, PATH to /bin:/usr/bin, the
              current directory becomes the user's home (/ if it
              is missing), and the shell starts as a login shell
              (argv[0] -sh), so it reads /etc/profile and
              $HOME/.profile.

       -c command
              run command with the shell's -c instead of an
              interactive shell; su's exit status is then the
              command's.

EXAMPLES
       $ su
       Password:
       # /etc/adduser alice
       # exit
       $ su guest          (no password: none asked)
       $ su - ken          (as if ken had logged in)
       $ su -c 'df' root   (one command as root)

FILES
       /etc/passwd    the accounts

EXIT STATUS
       Only on failure (otherwise su becomes the shell): 1 for an
       unknown user (su: no user name), a wrong password, a failed
       setuid, or a shell that cannot be executed; 2 for an unknown
       option.

SEE ALSO
       login(1), passwd(1), id(1), whoami(1), sh(1), passwd(5)

BUGS
       Without -, $USER and $HOME still name the old user.
       Nothing is logged.
